 - [KEY BUGFIX] - #1656: two `requirement_spec_mgr` methods were still left on the pre-2.0.1
  `req_specs` table shape and could not execute at all - `get_all_in_testproject()`
  (`lib/functions/requirement_spec_mgr.class.php:362`) and `get_by_title()` (`:798`) both
  selected `scope,total_req,type,author_id,creation_ts,modifier_id,modification_ts` from
  `req_specs`, but on 2.0.1 that table is only `(id,testproject_id,doc_id)` and those 7
  per-revision columns live in `req_specs_revisions`, so every call raised
  `1054 - Unknown column 'RSPEC.scope' in 'SELECT'` and `database::exec_query()` **die()d**
  (`lib/functions/database.class.php:224`) - the legacy reorder screen
  (`lib/requirements/reqSpecCommands.class.php:343` -> `reqSpecReorder.tpl`) answered with a
  raw `DB Access Error` backtrace page (absolute-path disclosure) instead of the drag&drop
  tree, and, far more widely than the issue reported, the SRS **create/edit** path died too,
  because `check_title()` -> `check_main_data()` -> `create()`/`update()` route through
  `get_by_title()` - which also meant the duplicate-title guard was never enforced.
  A third defect sat in the same statement: the caller ordered by the undefined alias
  `REQ_SPEC.id` while the query aliases `RSPEC` (`1054 Unknown column 'REQ_SPEC.id' in
  'ORDER BY'`). Fix: both methods now join the LATEST revision, the same way the already
  migrated sibling `get_by_id()` (`:189`, via `get_last_child_info()` `:2203`) does -
  `LEFT JOIN req_specs_revisions RSPEC_REV ON RSPEC_REV.parent_id = RSPEC.id AND
  RSPEC_REV.revision = (SELECT MAX(RSPEC_REV2.revision) FROM req_specs_revisions RSPEC_REV2
  WHERE RSPEC_REV2.parent_id = RSPEC.id)` (`LEFT JOIN`, not `JOIN`, so a spec without
  revision rows is still listed as before); `RSPEC`/`NH` aliases preserved so the caller's
  `ORDER BY NH.node_order` keeps working, `testproject_id` qualified, `doc_id` + `revision`
  added, the duplicated `AND RSPEC.id=NH.id` collapsed into the single `JOIN`; and
  `reqSpecCommands.class.php:342` `REQ_SPEC.id` -> `RSPEC.id`. No i18n / BFF / template
  change - all strings come from the pre-existing `lang_get()` keys. Verified on a fresh
  DB: 22/22 regression cases (both methods, latest-revision resolution, `node_order`
  ordering, empty project, unknown title, `check_title` accept/reject, `create()` +
  `update()` + `delete()` lifecycle, and the already-migrated `get_by_id()` proven
  byte-identical against base commit `09d030016` in a throwaway worktree), plus the live
  UI: the reorder screen lists both specs, the real create form saves, a duplicate
  title/doc_id is rejected, and `SELECT count(*) FROM events WHERE source='DATABASE'`
  is **0** (5 error events pre-fix). Post-code-review the latest-revision resolution was
  switched from `MAX(revision_id)` to `MAX(revision)` in a correlated subquery, so the two
  legacy methods now agree with `get_last_child_info()` (`:2203`) **and** with the
  modernized BFF (`api/reqreorder/index.php:157`, `api/reqspec/index.php:335,880`) instead
  of possibly disagreeing on the `scope`/`total_req` of one spec; the correlated form also
  lets the optimizer walk the unique `(parent_id,revision)` index instead of aggregating
  the whole revisions table, and both docblocks were completed with the `doc_id` /
  `revision` / `node_order` keys plus a trusted-literal warning on the raw `order_by`
  argument. Suite extended from 19 to 22 cases — adds the orphan-spec case (reachable
  because `create()` inserts `req_specs` + `nodes_hierarchy` *before* `create_revision()`,
  which is why the join must stay a `LEFT JOIN`) and the cross-layer agreement check
  (legacy `revision=2 scope="…rev2"` == BFF `revision=2 scope="…rev2"`). Three unrelated
  pre-existing defects found while testing were filed, not fixed: #1657 (`get_metrics()`
  `:318` has the same drift, dead code), #1658 (legacy create screen logs 2 E_WARNING per
  load on `reqSpecEdit.tpl:203,205`) and #1659 (the Postgres installer creates
  `req_specs_revisions_uidx1` on the wrong table `req_revisions`, so `(parent_id,revision)`
  uniqueness is not enforced on PostgreSQL). (Fixes #1656)
 - [KEY BUGFIX] - #1630: the Assign Test Project Roles and Assign Test Plan Roles
  grids labelled a FUSED full-name column "Last Name". The 2.0.1 rewrite split
  the legacy single user cell (`gui/templates/dashio/usermanagement/
  usersAssign.tpl:242`, `{$user->login} ({$user->firstName} {$user->lastName})`)
  into a Login + Name pair of columns but bound the Name `<th>` to the
  last-name-only i18n key, while the cell holds one fused display name
  (`api/roles/index.php:785` and `:949` -> `$u->getDisplayName()` ->
  `lib/functions/tlUser.class.php:525-533` -> `config.inc.php:749`
  `username_format = '%first% %last%'`) - so the header read "Last Name" over
  cells reading "Ann Designer" / "Testlink Administrator" in every locale. Added
  a dedicated `user.fullName` key to all 10 locale bundles and repointed
  `gui/templates/usermanagement/usersAssignProject.html:86` and
  `usersAssignPlan.html:88` at it; fr/ro use the explicit "Nom complet" /
  "Nume complet" because plain "Nom"/"Nume" is the surname label in those
  languages and would have left the defect unfixed (only invisible) there.
  `usersView.html:106-107` deliberately keeps `user.firstName` /
  `user.lastName` - that grid really does show two separate columns. No BFF
  change (the payload was always correct). Verified live on both grids across
  all 10 locales plus the untouched `usersView.html` split columns, the i18n
  fallback and the `events` table (suite 14/14 PASS).

PROCESS / KEEPING THIS CHANGELOG UPDATED
----------------------------------------
This CHANGELOG must be updated by every run that lands feature work on the
default branch:
- Modernized screen -> add one line under the matching section above (or the
  screen list) with its BFF and issue reference.
- Bugfix / security fix -> add one line under KEY BUGFIX with the issue number.
- New feature -> add one line under NEW FEATURES.
- New BFF endpoint -> keep count (api/*/index.php) in the BFF API LAYER line.
The rule is mirrored in ai/AGENTS.md (rule of the same name) and is enforced
during code review before each commit. A full per-issue history stays available
in docs/ and the GitHub Wiki.

