  project's name cannot linger through the in-flight window), on the combo `change`
  handler including the cleared-placeholder branch, on `loadUsers()` (before the async
  row load), and in all three neutralisation paths — `showDisabled()`, `showNoAccess()`,
  `showSessionExpired()` — which legacy also reached without rendering a table at all
  (`usersAssign.tpl:142` → `:295`). Identical helper shape to the sibling plan screen's
  `updatePlanRoleHeading()` (issue #1644), so both assignment screens read the same way.
- New i18n key `header.projectRoleHeading` in ALL 10 bundles, seeded from each locale's
  own `$TLS_th_roles_testproject` (`de` Testprojekt Rolle, `fr` Rôle de projet,
  `it` Ruoli in Test Project, `ja` テストプロジェクトでの役割, `ru` Роль в тест-проекте,
  `zh` 测试产品角色, …); `ro_RO` ships no `th_roles_*` entry at all, so `ro` mirrors the
  wording pattern of the existing `ro` `header.planRoleHeading`
  (`Rol în Proiectul de Test`). No BFF change — `meta/tproject-roles` already returned
  `projects[{id,name}]`; the name was simply never read outside the option text.
  Verified in-browser: default load, in-page combo switch, deep link, cleared combo,
  `showSessionExpired()`, `de` locale and a hostile `A&B <draft>` name all correct.
### Gap closed — Assign Test Plan Roles: the legacy role column was not sortable (Fixes #1670)
- `gui/templates/usermanagement/usersAssignPlan.html`: the `Plan Role Override`
  column carried `orderable: false` in `renderUsersTable()`'s `columnDefs`, so the
  header was dead (`sorting_disabled`, `aoColumns[4].bSortable === false`) and the
  administrator could not group the user list by assigned role. Legacy
  `gui/templates/dashio/usermanagement/usersAssign.tpl:111-120` only ever set
  `{"searchable": false, "targets": 1}` — no `orderable:false` anywhere — so the
  2-column legacy grid sorted by BOTH columns, and its sibling
  `usersAssignProject.html:505-508` already keeps the role column sortable.
- Restored orderability while keeping the column non-searchable (legacy parity), and
  gave it a real sort key: the cell holds a `<select>`, so DataTables' default string
  key is every `<option>` label concatenated — all rows share one suffix and only the
  first label ("<inherited> x" / "revert to inherited x") differs, i.e. an
  effectively arbitrary order. `$.fn.dataTable.ext.order['tlu-role-select']` (the
  documented DataTables extension point for "user editable elements such as form
  inputs", dt.js:14487 — the core registry is empty) now returns the selected option's
  label in data-index order, so clicking the header truly groups the list by assigned
  role and follows a live edit, an `onRoleChange()` cell re-sync and an
  `applyBulkRole()` re-render. The third-click default-order reset (:158-168) fires
  for the column too. No BFF change, no new i18n key. Suite 1670 9/9 PASS.
### Gap closed — legacy Test Plan Milestones grid never got DataTables (Fixes #1650)
- `gui/templates/dashio/plan/planMilestonesView.tpl:36` included
  `DataTables.inc.tpl` with `DataTablesSelector="#item_view"`, but the template's only
  candidate element was `<table class="common" width="100%">` with NO id, so
  `$('#item_view').DataTable(config)` ran on an empty jQuery set and was a silent
  no-op (no console error): the page-length menu, global search, column sorting and
  the record counter were all absent, and the `lengthMenu`/`stateSave` config emitted
  into the page by the include was never applied. The `[planMilestonesView]`
  `item_view_table` / `item_view_thead` / `pagination_length` config added by #1594
  had no consumer.
- Ported the grid to the Dashio list-screen contract already used by the sibling
  templates (`buildView.tpl:86`, `planView.tpl:96`, `rolesView.tpl:73`,
  `projectView.tpl:84`): `<table class="{#item_view_table#}" id="item_view">`, header
  row wrapped in `<thead class="{#item_view_thead#}">`, data rows in `<tbody>`
  (DataTables needs the thead/tbody split to build its column model), and the Delete
  action column marked `data-orderable="false" class="icon_cell"` so it stays out of
  sorting and out of the global search index. The separate "Milestones Report"
  (`simple_tableruler`) table is untouched, per the issue scope.
- Browser-verified on both project variants: `testPriorityEnabled=1` (7 columns) and
  `testPriorityEnabled=0` (5 columns). `#item_view` = 1, class
  `table table-bordered dataTable no-footer`, thead `thead-dark`, `#item_view_wrapper`
  present, `.dataTables_length` (20/40/60/All) / `.dataTables_filter` /
  `.dataTables_info` ("Showing 1 to 3 of 3 entries") all present, search filters
  ("Gamma" -> 1 of 1 filtered from 3), Name sorting asc/desc works, `stateSave` now
  persists the page length across reloads, delete `delete_confirmation()` handlers and
  `planMilestonesEdit.php` links unchanged. Reference screen `planView.php` in the same
  session measured identically, proving the contract. Console clean, Event Viewer
  0 new ERROR/WARNING (all 11 rows are log_level 16 audit). Suite 1650 14/14 PASS.

### Key bugfix — 'Assign Requirements' modal on Test Specification was always empty (Refs #1598)
- `gui/templates/testcases/testSpec.html`: `openAssignReqs(tcaseId)` accepted the test-case id
  the button passes and never stored it, so the module-level `arqTcaseId` stayed `0` for the whole
  session. The guard `if (!specId || !arqTcaseId) return;` in `arqLoadReqs()` therefore
  short-circuited **before** issuing `GET /assign-reqs`, and `arqDoAssign()` would have posted
  `tcase_id=0` (rejected server-side by `api/requirements/index.php`). Result: the
  FREE/ASSIGNED REQUIREMENTS lists were always empty while the specification combo above them was
  correctly populated — a functional-looking dead end, so the legacy "link a requirement to a single
  test case" feature had no working entry point. The backend was measured healthy (200 with both
  requirements `unassigned` for `tcase_id=9`); the button only became reachable through the gate
  restored in #1596, which is why the omission stayed invisible until now. The id is now stored on
  open (`arqTcaseId = parseInt(tcaseId || 0, 10) || 0`), the state (`arqTcaseId`, `arqSpecs` and
  every list/counter/hint) is reset on close via the new `closeAssignReqs()` so a stale id can
  never be reused for a different test case, the shared clear-on-open/close block is extracted to
  `arqResetUi()`, and `arqDoAssign()` refuses to POST with a localised
  `reqAssign.noTestCase` message (added to all 10 locale bundles) instead of silently posting
  `tcase_id=0`. The `openAssignReqs()` of `tcView.html` / `tcEdit.html` are separate, argument-less
  functions and were deliberately left untouched.
### Key bugfix — requirement log messages were echoed as unescaped HTML (Refs #1652, #1653, #1654, #1655)
- `lib/ajax/getreqspeclog.php` and `lib/ajax/getreqlog.php` did `SELECT log_message FROM
  <table> WHERE id = <intval>` and `echo`ed the stored RichEdit/TinyMCE blob straight into the page:
  **no rights check** and **no proof the id belonged to the caller's test project**, so ANY project's
  requirement log was returned to ANY authenticated user, and a log containing markup executed in the
  caller's DOM. Both now answer the contract each live caller actually has - the six legacy
  `Ext.ToolTip` `autoLoad` call sites (`reqSpecCompareRevisions.tpl:34`, `reqSpecViewRevision.tpl:31`,
  `include/reqSpecViewJS.inc.tpl:30`, `reqViewVersions.tpl:174`, `reqViewRevisionRO.tpl:28`,
  `reqCompareVersions.tpl:34`) get an **HTML-escaped fragment**, while a plain browser deep link gets a
  302 to the modern screen, which enforces `mgt_view_req` on the **owning** project (resolved from
  the database) and renders the log with `.text()`. The fragment branch authorizes too: it derives the
  owning project from the id itself (the live tooltip callers pass `item_id` only) and answers **403**
  without the log body when the user has no `mgt_view_req` there, so the cross-project read that
  #1652 found is closed on **both** contracts.
- The new BFF also had three defects of its own, all found by testing: an unknown id answered **500
  `db_error`** instead of 404 because `get_recordset()` returns `null` for both an empty result and a
  failed query (#1653); three nonexistent `name` columns (`req_specs.name`, `requirements.name`,
  `testprojects.name`) 500'd **every** happy path - titles live on `nodes_hierarchy` and
  `testproject::getName()` (#1653); and the `parent_name` reads left behind by that fix raised
  **44 `E_WARNING` rows** that only the Event Viewer could see (#1654). The context row also showed the
  test project under a label reading "Requirement specification" in all 10 locales (#1655).
