### Screen — Test Case Tree Navigator `tcProjectTree` (Refs #1770)
- Modernizes the last unauthorized test case tree loader, `lib/ajax/gettprojectnodes.php`, which
  answered the children of ONE expanded ExtJS node for the LEFT FRAME of the 1.9.20 work areas
  "add/remove test cases" (`planAddTC_m1.tpl`), "update test plan TC assignments" (`planUpdateTC.tpl`),
  "test urgency" (`planUrgency.tpl`) and "execution assignment" (`tc_exec_assignment.tpl`). It ran
  `testlinkInitPage()` - a SESSION check only - and then built the `project -> suite -> test case`
  JSON straight out of `root_node` / `node` / `filter_node` read from `$_REQUEST`, with no rights
  check and no project scope: **any** authenticated user, including one with no test case right,
  could read the suite names, test case names and `tc_external_id`s of **any** test project by
  sending arbitrary ids. The file also walked out of the test specification (it filtered on
  `parent_id` alone) and recursed once per suite level with an unbounded `parent_id IN (...)` list.
  Same class as #1696 (`getrequirementnodes.php`) and #1765 (`getreqcoveragenodes.php`).
- New Dashio screen `gui/templates/testcases/tcProjectTree.html` backed by the read-only BFF
  `api/tcprojecttree/index.php` (`GET ?action=projects|init|children|filter`): lazy-loaded
  project -> suite -> test case tree with a **readable-project switcher** (the legacy frame always
  used the session project and had none), live recursive case counts, a "Name (n)" folder label, the
  `treemenu_show_testcase_id` external-id column, the `show_tcases` and `filter_node` gestures, deep
  links per row (project info / test specification / test case viewer), and the six state cards.
- The BFF checks `mgt_view_tc|mgt_modify_tc` on the ADDRESSED project **before** resolving it, so an
  unauthorized caller gets the same `403 no_right` for a real and a bogus project id (#1697 pattern),
  and `needOwnedNode()` proves every requested node id to live under that project root before a
  single name is returned - a foreign suite or test case answers `404 node_not_found`, the same as
  a non-existent id. Legacy `filter_node` semantics are reproduced exactly: the filter narrows the
  ROOT children only (`parent == root_node`, `gettprojectnodes.php:99`).
- The legacy endpoint is kept as a **non-mutating** session-guarded shim: GET/HEAD answer `302` to
  the modern screen (carrying `root_node` -> `tproject_id`, `filter_node`, `show_tcases`; the read is
  deliberately not replayed because it was never authorized), a write verb answers `405` pointing at
  the new API. It had no live caller left: the work-area frames render the tree server-side through
  `inc_refreshTreeWithFilters.tpl` and nothing instantiates `tlTestCaseFilterControl` any more, the
  same retirement #1660 did for `lib/ajax/dragdroptprojectnodes.php`.
- `tcpt.*` (45 keys) + `footers.tcProjectTree` in all 10 TLi18n bundles and a new
  `$actions->tcProjectTree` in `lib/functions/common.php`. Suite 1770: **128 checks PASS**
  (`tmp/suite_1770.py`), 0 new Event Viewer Error/Warning rows, 0 console messages.
- **#1771** - `tc_external_id` came back empty for every test case: a test case VERSION node hangs off
  the **test case**, never off the suite (the legacy query needed its third join for exactly that),
  so the suite-level version filter matched nothing. The BFF now joins suite -> case -> version and
  pins `MAX(version node id)`, which also removes the legacy `SELECT DISTINCT` ambiguity about which
  version supplies the id.
- **#1772** - the Hide/Show test cases toggle and the suite focus changed the tree but never reached
  the address bar, so a reload silently reverted them (`resetTree()` was only called by the project
  switcher). Both gestures are now URL state and survive a reload.
- **#1773** - "Expand all" stopped after the first level, because only the suites already known to
  the client were flagged. A sticky mode plus `expandInto()` now walks cached and freshly loaded rows
  to the leaves; "Collapse all" and a manual twisty click switch it off again.
- **#1774** (security, found by the code review) - the children query excluded node types with a
  hand-typed denylist `(4, 5, 6, 8)` where the legacy loader excluded by DESCRIPTION
  `testcase_version, testplan, requirement_spec, requirement` = `4, 5, 6, 7`. A `requirement` row
  (7) hung under a test project was therefore returned to callers holding only `mgt_view_tc` -
  proven by planting one and reading it back. The query now **allow-lists** the two kinds of row the
  tree can render (`testsuite`, `testcase`), so a node type nobody thought of cannot leak; the suite
  plants one node of each of the six hidden types and proves none comes back.

### KEY BUGFIX — 14 BFF helpers: `out($data, $code = 200)` reset the HTTP status, so every error branch answered HTTP 200 (Fixes #1776)

The `out()`-family helpers in `api/execassignment`, `execassignmentcopy`, `ltx`, `logviewer`,
`suitemove`, `tcassign2tplan`, `tcassignments`, `tcbulkop`, `tcprintlaunch`, `tcreorder`,
`tcstepsreorder`, `tcsummary`, `tcunassignall`, `testcasesedit` set `http_response_code($code)`
unconditionally, so the `$code = 200` default silently overwrote the status the caller had
already set one line earlier. Every `http_response_code(N); out([...]);` branch of those
endpoints — **25 live sites** (`execassignment` 13, `tcassignments` 11, `tcassign2tplan` 1) —
answered **HTTP 200 with an error body**: validation failures, unknown routes and rights
denials were indistinguishable from successes for `fetch` (`res.ok`), DataTables ajax error
handling and any monitoring keyed on the status line. The helper now only sets the status when
a code is passed explicitly (`out($data, $code = null)` + `if (!is_null($code))`), the guard
`api/keywordsxml/index.php:86` already shipped. The 154 call sites that pass the code as the
second argument are unaffected, success responses stay 200, and the 401 session guards (which
never reach `out()`) are unaffected. `api/logviewer/index.php:82` carried the same latent
signature; its DB-error 500 branch at `:332-337` happens to pass `, 500)` explicitly, so it
was not one of the 25 broken sites — it is aligned here only to remove the trap for future
callers. Endpoints whose `out()` takes no `$code` at all (`mainpage`, `plans`, `results`,
`suiteview`, `users`, `requirements`, `testcases`, …) never touch the status line and are not
affected. Regression suite: `bash tmp/verify_1776.sh` (34 PASS / 0 FAIL).
### KEY BUGFIX (access control) — `tlUser::hasRight()` denied a plan-scoped right on EVERY 3-argument call, admin included (Fixes #1769, Fixes #1775)
- One line, `lib/functions/tlUser.class.php:962`, reported twice and fixed once: #1769 and #1775 are
  the same defect.
- **What was wrong.** The private-TEST-PLAN guard read `$accessPublic['tplan']`, but that key is only
  ever filled when the caller opts in with the 5th argument `$getAccess === true`. Since **#1763** made
  `$accessPublic` unconditionally an array on a 3-argument call - the shape all 763 `hasRight()` call
  sites use, 100+ of them under `api/` - the old `!is_null($accessPublic)` test stopped short-circuiting
  and the branch went live for callers that never requested the flag. Two consequences:
  1. `E_WARNING Undefined array key "tplan"` on every such call: **51 identical Event Viewer rows from
     a single login**, drowning every real warning.
  2. **A rights denial, not a harmless notice.** PHP evaluates `null == 0` as **TRUE**, so the branch
     returned `false` for every user holding no `user_testplan_roles` row for that plan - **including
     admin**, which has no plan-role row either. #1769 measured `admin` going `200 -> 403` on
     `api/attachments?action=list&table=executions`. #1775 classified this as "no security impact,
     null == 0 is false"; that premise is wrong and the fix below treats it as an access-control defect.
- **The fix.** Guard the KEY with `isset()`, exactly as the sibling TEST PROJECT read at `:942` already
  does, so the branch is dead unless the caller actually asked for the plan accessibility flag.
  3-argument calls regain their pre-#1763 semantics; the 29 call sites that pass `$getAccess = true`
  keep the guard in full.
- **Verified** (`tmp/verify_1775.php`, 16 checks; `tmp/verify_1775_guarded.php`): plan-role user and
  no-plan-role user and admin all granted on a public plan via the 3-argument shape with **0 warnings**;
  a **private** plan still denies for the `$getAccess = true` callers (`GUARD PRESERVED`); 50 consecutive
  plan-scoped 3-argument calls add **0** rows to `events`. The suite was re-run with the fix reverted and
  fails exactly the 4 predicted checks (`false` + 1 warning each for the no-plan-role user and for admin),
  so it genuinely detects the defect rather than passing vacuously.

### KEY BUGFIX (security) — the two reorder BFFs answered `403` for a foreign container / test case version, so ANY logged-in user could enumerate the node tree of EVERY test project (Fixes #1761, Fixes #1762)
- **The leak needed no rights at all.** `nodes_hierarchy` ids are global across all test projects, and
  both endpoints resolved the caller-supplied id to its OWNING project and compared that owner against
  the requested project *before* asking whether the caller had any right on the owner
  (`api/tcreorder` `tcreoProject()`, `api/tcstepsreorder` `tsroContext()`). So a valid session with
  zero rights anywhere could tell three cases apart per id: **does not exist** (`404 Container not found`),
  **exists and is foreign** (`403 … belongs to another test project`), and — via the distinct wording —
  **exists but is not a container / is an orphan / is a different node type**. That is an existence
  oracle *and* an ownership-membership oracle over the whole `nodes_hierarchy` id space, sweepable
  without a single test-project grant. `api/tcstepsreorder` leaked it on all four entry points that
  authorize through `tsroContext()`: the read (`mgt_view_tc`) and the three writes (`mgt_modify_tc`).
- **The fix.** Every refusal caused by a caller-supplied object id is now ONE opaque HTTP 404,
  **byte-identical** to the answer for a non-existent id, whatever the real reason — missing, wrong
  `node_type`, orphan, foreign project, or the caller simply has no rights. New single-source helpers
  `tcreoNoSuchContainer()` and `tsroNoSuchVersion()` are the only ways those branches answer.
  Three follow-on collapses were required to make the `node_id` axis un-bypassable:
  `tcreoRequireChildTestcase()` (4 branches), `tcreoContainerChildren()` (3 branches) and the
  move-boundary `array_search` straggler all now emit the same `Node not found` / `Container not found`
  body as the absent case. The project-level `init` (no object id) legitimately keeps an informative
  403, and that 403 is now itself identical for a non-existent project.
- **Deliberate trade-off.** A view-only user of their OWN project is refused with the opaque 404 rather
  than "access denied", because the guard is armed whenever a container / version id is supplied rather
  than only when the caller also named a project. The narrower rule leaks: a caller holding nothing
  anywhere must not learn that an id exists either, and that is the case that actually matters (the first
  version of the fix shipped this gap and the suite caught it, cases R20/R22). The cost is one
  "not found" card for a user who can only read, on a screen that is linked for modifiers anyway.
- **A second, pre-existing leak found by the mandatory code review (rule 16) in the same file.**
  `?action=versions` resolves a *project*, not a node id, and trusted `tlUser::hasRight()` — which does
  **not** deny an id that resolves to nothing: it falls through to the GLOBAL role and answers *yes*.
  So for a default global Test Designer (`role_id = 4`) with no `user_testproject_roles` row, the gate
  refused an **existing** private project with `403` while letting `424242` through to
  `tsroVersions()`, which answered `404 Test project not found` — a live test-project existence oracle
  over the whole `testprojects` id space. The sibling `tcreoProject()` was already immune because it
  checks the project row itself. Fixed by having `tsroVersions()` answer the same 403 for a project that
  does not exist.
- **Verified** (`tmp/fixtures_1761.php` + `tmp/verify_1761.php`, **95/95 PASS**; 0 new
  ERROR/WARNING rows in `events`). The suite was re-run with each file reverted and fails exactly the
  predicted checks (`45 PASS / 50 FAIL` on pre-fix code, and `403 vs 404` on the new project-axis cases
  when only `api/tcstepsreorder` is reverted), so it genuinely detects the defect rather than passing
  vacuously. A `sm1761designer` user (global role 4) was added to the fixture for the project axis
  because an all-global-role-3 fixture provably makes that case vacuous — `hasRight(right, 424242)` is
  false for such users, so absent and forbidden collapse onto one answer.
- **Follow-up filed, NOT fixed here: #1779** — `api/suitemove` arms its #1759 leak guard only when the
  *project* id is caller-supplied, so `?tproject_id=<any>&container_id=<container of an unentitled
  project>` still answers `403` while an absent container answers `404`. Confirmed with curl, not
  inferred. Suite 1759 rows `M12`/`M12c` pin today's behaviour and would need updating with that fix.
### KEY BUGFIX (security) — `lib/ajax/stepReorder.php` renumbered `tcsteps.step_number` for ANY logged-in caller (role *no rights* included) over a plain GET, CSRF-able, and interpolated the step ids into the UPDATE unescaped (Fixes #1673)

The 1.9.20 orphan behind the never-shipped TableDnD step drag-and-drop performed
`// No authorization checks`, read `$_REQUEST` (a GET mutated state, no token) and handed the
caller-supplied ids straight to `testcase::set_step_number()` — the same SQL-injection sink now
proven by `tsroRequireOwnSteps()` in the modern BFF. The endpoint is retired: `GET`/`HEAD` `302`
to the modern **Reorder Test Case Steps** screen (the mutation is deliberately *not* replayed),
every write verb is `405`, and the Event Viewer trails each refusal. Replacement write path is the
authorized `POST /api/tcstepsreorder/index.php` (`bffSameOriginGuard` + `bffEnforceSession` +
`mgt_modify_tc` on the owning project + strict step-ownership proof). Regression suite
`tmp/verify_1673.php` = 29/29 PASS, and it fails 7 cases (including a DB-level corruption assert)
on the pre-fix code. See `docs/Bugfix-Issue-1673-stepReorder-Unauthenticated-CSRF-Step-Reorder.md`.
### KEY BUGFIX — Assign Test Plan Roles: the 2nd change of a row's role `<select>` threw `TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'row')`, leaving a stale Save button and a stale cell cache (Fixes #1680)

`gui/templates/usermanagement/usersAssignPlan.html` `onRoleChange()` re-synced the
DataTables cell cache with `assignDt.cell(tr, 4).data(...)`, passing the **jQuery
wrapper** `$(sel).closest('tr')`. DataTables 1.13.7 only takes its exact, index-based
row-selector path when the selector carries a `nodeName`
(`jquery.dataTables.js:8116`); a jQuery wrapper falls through to the generic catch-all,
which re-resolves the row by a strict node-identity scan of `aoData[..].nTr`
(`dt.js:8165-8179`). A miss there makes `rows()`/`cells()` return an **empty** API
instance, and the following `cell().data()` dereferences `this[0].row` on it →
`Uncaught TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'row')`
(`jquery.dataTables.min.js:4:66727`). The exception escaped the handler, so the row
highlight, the *Modified* badge and `$('#saveBtn').prop('disabled', !isDirty())` were
never applied and the cached cell survived the next paging/search/sort re-draw stale.
The call site now passes the DOM node (`tr[0]`) plus a `tr.length` guard, and only the
best-effort cell refresh is wrapped in `try/catch` — the `users` model stays the source
of truth for the Save payload, so a refresh that cannot be applied must never abort the
save-state update (a stale Save button is the worse failure). No BFF, i18n or schema
change. Verified against the 9-case regression matrix, including a full Save round trip
(`user_testplan_roles.role_id` persisted) and a clean Event Viewer.

### Screen — Copy Requirement Specification popup `reqSpecCopy` (Refs #1797)

Modern screen `gui/templates/requirements/reqSpecCopy.html` + BFF `api/reqspeccopy/index.php` (`GET ?action=init&req_spec_id=N[&tproject_id=P]`, `GET ?action=projects`, `POST ?action=copy&req_spec_id=N&container_id=M[&tproject_id=P]&target_position=top|bottom`), replacing the `copy` / `doCopy` actions of `lib/requirements/reqSpecEdit.php` + `gui/templates/dashio/requirements/reqSpecCopy.tpl`. Two reasons this screen was worth restoring: **it had no entry point at all** (both legacy viewer templates only *assigned* `{$req_spec_copy_url}` — `dashio:53`, `tl-classic:50` — and never used it, and `reqSpecViewButtons.inc.tpl` has no such button, so the popup was URL-only even in 1.9.20; it now gets a real toolbar button) and **the legacy write was unsafe** — `checkRights()` saw only the SESSION context while `containerID` / `tproject_id` came straight out of the POST body, so a copy could be aimed at any node of any test project. Hardened over legacy: `mgt_view_req` + `mgt_modify_req` on the destination project, `mgt_view_req` on the project owning the source, the destination node *proved* to be a test project / requirement specification of that project, a destination inside the source subtree refused, the dead `target_position` top/bottom radio actually honoured (two-phase collision-free `node_order` renumbering), and `lib/requirements/reqSpecEdit.php` `copy`/`doCopy` reduced to a **non-mutating 302 shim** carrying `?legacy_intent=`. New **Copy Requirement Specification** button on the modern Requirement Specification Viewer + `$actions->reqSpecCopy`. i18n `rsc.*` (37) + `footers.reqSpecCopy` in all 10 bundles. Suite 1797 = 25/25 PASS.

### KEY BUGFIX — `?action=projects` offered orphan test-project nodes that every copy was then refused for (Fixes #1798)

TestLink 2.0.1 keeps the test-project **name** in `nodes_hierarchy` and the project **row** in `testprojects` (`testprojects` has no `name` column), and the two drift — `testproject::delete()` can leave a `node_type_id=1` row behind with no `testprojects` row. The destination selector listed such an orphan, but `rscResolveContainer()` proves the destination against `testprojects`, so every pick of one died with 400 `invalid_destination`: a selector entry no request could ever satisfy. Now `INNER JOIN testprojects … AND TP.active = 1`. (Also corrects a 401 message that said `User found` instead of `User not found`.)

### KEY BUGFIX — the copy confirmation was wiped by its own refresh, and every error card showed the wrong text with an empty machine code (Fixes #1799)

`doCopy()` showed the success box and then called `load()` to refresh the destination list; `load()` started with `hideAll()`, which hid the message again — the copy really happened, the tree grew, and the user saw **no confirmation at all**. `showState()` resolved its text target as "the first `<p>` without a `<code>`", which on `#stDenied` is the localized **access-denied hint**, so a 404 answered as *"copying a requirement specification needs the mgt_view_req and mgt_modify_req rights"* with an **empty machine code**. A stale `?tproject_id=` (exactly what the legacy shim hands over — the SESSION id) was taken at face value and produced zero containers, an empty selector and a disabled Copy; it is now repaired to the project owning the specification (`tproject_fallback_from`), while a **real** project the caller cannot write into still gets its 403. The `?legacy_intent=` notice was wiped twice and threw `ReferenceError: p is not defined`. Explicit `#<name>Text` / `#<name>Code` hooks per state card, `hideAll(keepMsg)`, and the repair landed in both `init` and `copy`.

### KEY BUGFIX — Event Viewer: `E_WARNING Undefined property: tlUser::$id` per denial, and a warning pair per legacy copy shim hit (Fixes #1800)

`tlUser` has **no** `->id` property (the id is the protected `dbID` of `tlObject`, with no accessor), so every rights denial wrote an E_WARNING row and the log text read `for user 0` — misleading in the very log used to audit who was refused; now `$user->login`. The `copy` / `doCopy` shim also ran **after** `new reqSpecCommands()` / `initialize_gui()`, both of which walk the SESSION test project, so a session whose project had been deleted since raised `Trying to access array offset on null` for a request that was only ever going to redirect; the interception now sits right after `init_args()`. Verified: three consecutive shim hits add **0** rows to `events`.

### KEY BUGFIX — Copy to top/bottom renumbered the destination container by id, reversing the user's own order; and the copy popup had a stored-XSS sink (Fixes #1801, #1802)

Found by the mandatory code review of #1797. `api/reqspeccopy/index.php` :: `rscApplyTargetPosition()` collected the destination container's children as bare ids and `sort()`ed them, i.e. by **`nodes_hierarchy.id`**, while `node_order` is the real sort key everywhere — on the review fixture `node_order` order was `[167, 148]` while `148 < 167`, so **any copy renumbered them `[148=1, 167=2]` and silently reversed the arrangement the user had made**, rewriting rows that are none of the copy's business. It also renumbered only `node_type_id IN (1,6)`, so **requirement children were left behind** and the new spec could tie with a requirement on the same `(parent_id, node_order)`. Now every child is collected with its `node_order`, ordered by `(node_order ASC, id ASC)` and renumbered in two collision-free phases, with the `exec_query()` result actually checked (409 `position_write_failed`) and the default position corrected to `top` (#1801). In the same pass: the popup rendered the stored scope through `document.createElement('div').innerHTML`, which is **not** an inert sink — `<img src=x onerror=...>` in a specification's scope executed in every viewer's session (`window.__xss === true`); `stripHtml()` now parses with `DOMParser` (#1802). Also #1802: the real `copy_to()` failure reason was discarded behind a ternary whose two arms were both `copy_failed` (the non-transactional partial id is now returned too), the `legacy_intent` notice no longer overwrites a successful-copy confirmation, `out()` survives non-UTF-8 titles with `JSON_INVALID_UTF8_SUBSTITUTE` instead of an empty HTTP 200, the container selection survives the post-copy reload, and `footers.reqSpecCopy` is finally rendered.

### KEY BUGFIX — `requirement_spec_mgr::copy_to()` logged an `E_WARNING` on every recursive copy (Fixes #1803)

The subtree loop ran `$sdx <= count($subtree)`, one element past the end, so `$elem = &$subtree[$sdx]` and `$elem['node_type_id']` on `null` raised `Trying to access array offset on null` (line 1938) — an Event Viewer warning row on **every** recursive specification copy (also hit by reqSpecTreeCopy and the plan-level copies). Off-by-one fixed; verified 0 new `events` rows for a copy whose source has a child specification and requirements. Suite 1801 = 15/15 PASS.

**Review pass on the three fixes above** (mandatory re-review of the fix commit): **no blocker**, verdict *safe to merge*, and the two MAJORs it did find — introduced by the fix commit itself — are closed: the `copy_to()` failure path had lost its HTTP **409** (`failOut` replaced by a bare `out()` turned every failure into HTTP 200, and since the body arrived in the client's `.done()` branch the form stayed disabled and the popup was effectively bricked until a manual reload — the button is now re-enabled in that branch and the 409 restored), and `partial` read `$newSpecId`, which does not exist in that scope, so it was permanently 0; it is now `$op['id']`, the specification `copy_to()` actually committed. Also closed: the server-locale `copy_to()` sentence is mapped to a stable machine code (`duplicate_doc_id` / `error_creating_req_spec` / `error_updating_req_spec`) with the text kept in `message` and control characters stripped before `tLog` (the sentence embeds the user-controlled title — log forging), an explicitly empty `target_position` now follows the advertised `top` default, a container holding only the new copy is normalised to `node_order = 1`, `loadProjects()` gained the missing `.fail()` so a failed project list no longer leaves another project's containers on screen, and the dead `setFooter()` (it only re-set an attribute `TLi18n.apply()` had already applied, and it ran *after* it) is gone — the footer is driven by `data-i18n="footers.reqSpecCopy"` alone. Suite 1801 grown to 23/23 PASS.

### KEY BUGFIX (data integrity) — `api/platforms` DELETE removed a platform that was still linked to a test plan, orphaning the link (Fixes #1637)

`DELETE /api/platforms/index.php/{id}?tproject_id=N` asked `tlPlatform::getAll()` for the platform's
`linked_count`, but `getAll()` merges **its own** defaults over the caller's options
(`lib/functions/tlPlatform.class.php:277-283`) and two of those defaults are WHERE-clause **filters**:
`enable_on_execution => true` becomes `AND enable_on_execution = 1` and `is_open => true` becomes
`AND is_open = 1`; only an explicit `null` disables one (`:292-301`). So any platform with
`is_open = 0` **or** `enable_on_execution = 0` was simply **absent** from the returned set, the lookup
loop ended with `$linked = 0`, the `if ($linked > 0)` guard was never reached and
`tlPlatform::delete()` — a bare `DELETE FROM platforms WHERE id = …`, no cascade — ran anyway,
leaving a permanent orphan `testplan_platforms` row. Only the `is_open=1 / enable_on_execution=1`
combination was ever protected, and the failure was silent: HTTP `200 {"status":"ok"}`, no Event Viewer
row. The list route in the same file (`:132`) already passed the three `null` overrides, so `GET`
reported `deletable:false` for exactly the platforms `DELETE` happily removed — the two routes
contradicted each other about the same platform in the same second. Root cause is a BFF regression
rather than a legacy defect: 1.9.20 `platformsDelete.php` derived the rule from the platform's testplan
usage (`warning_cannot_delete_platform`), which no display flag can defeat; the rewrite reused a
*display* query as an *integrity guard*.

**Fix** (`api/platforms/index.php`, DELETE route): pass `'enable_on_design' => null,
'enable_on_execution' => null, 'is_open' => null` to `getAll()` — byte-for-byte the option set the list
route already used, so `GET` and `DELETE` now agree by construction. The guard additionally **fails
closed** (`500 DELETE_CHECK_FAILED`) instead of reading "row absent" as "not linked", removing the
exact fail-open assumption that caused the bug. No new SQL, no change to the shared legacy class, no
change to the other three `getAll()` callers (`lib/functions/specview.php:1421`,
`lib/functions/testproject.class.php:3129`, `lib/functions/tlPlatform.class.php:601`), and the `422`
body is byte-identical, so no i18n key and no frontend change were needed.

**Verified**: all 4 (`is_open × enable_on_execution`) combinations now answer `422 DELETE_BLOCKED`;
unlinked platforms still delete with `200` (not over-blocked); cross-project and unknown ids still
`404`; a project with no test plans still `200` (empty guard set does not 500); multi-testplan counting
intact (`linked_count: 2`, both links preserved after the `422`); **0 orphan `testplan_platforms` rows**
after a full run (pre-fix: 2); `php -l` clean; Event Viewer shows no new Error/Warning/Notice row; the
modernized `platformsView.html` screen still renders with every linked row's delete control disabled.
Suite `T1637` in `tmp/TLU_Test_Cases.md` — 17/17 PASS. Restored from branch `fix/issue-1637`
(`716d96587` code, `8ac6cc966` suite + this entry, `e255cb5f7` `docs/` mirror); the code, the suite
and the `docs/` page had already reached `sebiboga`, only this CHANGELOG entry was still missing.
- Execution Notes popup shim (lib/execute/execNotes.php) converted to session-guarded redirect to modern screen `execNotes.html` + in-process BFF proxy; added `footers.execNotes` in all 10 i18n bundles (Refs #1857).
### Screen — Execution Notes, read-only viewer `execNotesReadonly` (Refs #1807)
- Screen: `gui/templates/execute/execNotesReadonly.html`, a standalone Dashio page
  (`gui/templates/execute/execNotesReadonly.html?exec_id=<execution id>`) backed by the new
  BFF `api/execnotesreadonly/index.php` with two safe-GET routes: `?action=view` (JSON payload
  for the screen) and `?action=fragment` (escaped `text/html` for the legacy `url2load()`
  call sites). Replaces `lib/execute/getExecNotes.php` +
  `gui/templates/dashio/execute/getExecNotes.tpl`. The note is never shown without its
  context: test case + `#PREFIX-N` chip, suite, plan, project, build, platform, status badge
  and execution timestamp. The stored RichEdit blob is flattened server-side to PLAIN TEXT
  (`<br>` and block ends become newlines, everything else stripped, entities decoded once) and
  lands in the page via `textContent`, so a stored payload cannot execute. "Open editable
  notes" is enabled only when the BFF reports `can_edit`, and the button fails closed on every
  non-ok state (missing id / 401 / 403 / 404 / 400) so it can never launch
  `execNotes.html?exec_id=<bad id>`. Right enforced on the OWNING test project, resolved by
  walking `executions -> testplans -> testproject` and never from a client-supplied id
  (`exec_ro_access` / `exec_edit_notes` / `testplan_execute`, identical to `api/execnotes`), and
  the executed tcversion is proved to live in that same project by walking
  `nodes_hierarchy.parent_id` to the `node_type_id = 1` root - both fail CLOSED with 404
  rather than fall back to the session project or show a foreign test case name. 29 `enro.*`
  keys in all ten `gui/templates/i18n/*.json` bundles. Canonical action
  `$actions->execNotesReadonly` in `lib/functions/common.php`. No ASIDE entry: with no
  `exec_id` the screen can only render a "pick an execution" state, so a menu link would be a
  dead end - the screen is deep-linked instead.
- KEY BUGFIX (security) - #1808: the legacy `getExecNotes.php` authorized NOTHING beyond
  "a session exists" (`testlinkInitPage()` was never called, so no right and no ownership
  were checked) and handed a bare `?exec_id=` straight to the view: any authenticated user,
  role 3 `<no rights>` included, could read the notes of ANY execution on ANY test project by
  enumerating `?exec_id=`. It also dereferenced `$map[0]['notes']` with no guard (E_WARNING +
  fatal 500 on an unknown id) and rendered the stored RichEdit blob through the web editor, a
  stored payload executing with the app origin's privileges. The controller is now a
  non-mutating shim preserving the legacy contract - 401 with no session, 405 for a write
  verb, 400 for a bad id, 302 to the modern screen for a browser navigation, and 200
  `text/html` from the BFF for the XHR fragment - and the fragment branch additionally
  requires a SAME-ORIGIN caller, because the four legacy call sites (`dashio`/`tl-classic`
  `execSetResultsUtils`, `execSetResultsJS`, `execHistory`) assign the answer into
  `innerHTML`; `Origin`/`Referer` are validated authoritatively and the `X-Requested-With`
  hint never overrides a foreign `Origin` (issue #1679). The shim `require`s the BFF
  IN-PROCESS rather than proxying it over HTTP, because the fragment must keep the same
  session cookie, status codes and body and a nested self-request is not available (the dev
  server is single-threaded, so it deadlocks into a 502). Also fixed while exercising the
  screen in the browser (commit `774abca0f`): the success handler aliased the payload with
  `var x = r.execution || {}` while `can_edit` is a ROOT-level field, so "Open editable
  notes" was permanently disabled; the edit button stayed clickable in every non-ok state;
  and a refused read was logged at WARNING, so every denied user added a new Warning to the
  Event Viewer (now AUDIT). Verified: role-3 `norights1807` gets `403 no_right` on BOTH the
  modern and the legacy-fragment path with no note leaked; stored `<img onerror>` payload
  lands as inert text (`window.__enro_xss` undefined, 0 child elements in the notes box);
  Event Viewer shows no new Error/Warning rows. The mandatory code review (subagent) then reported **no blockers** and **2 MAJOR**
items, both fixed: (a) the 403-vs-404 split was an **existence oracle** - any
authenticated user could enumerate `?exec_id=` and learn which executions exist across
every test project, the same ruling as #1792 - so "no right" now answers byte-identically
to "no such execution" and the client's Access-denied card is kept only as a safety net;
(b) the BOM strip used a `/u` `preg_replace()` that returns **NULL** for invalid UTF-8
(a Latin-1 byte, a truncated sequence - exactly what a 1.9.20 database carries), and that
NULL blanked the whole note, so the screen claimed "No execution notes recorded." for an
execution that had notes; the strip is now byte-mode and `json_encode` carries
`JSON_INVALID_UTF8_SUBSTITUTE` (without the flag the same payload encoded to **0 bytes**).
Also from the review: the verb check moved ABOVE `bffSameOriginGuard()` so the documented
405 is reachable for every caller instead of being swallowed as 403; `fail()` renamed
`enro_fail()` because this file is co-included by the shim; the shim's two same-origin
refusals now answer a JSON machine code (`cross_origin` / `unparseable_origin`) instead
of an empty page; both file headers corrected from "4 legacy call sites" to the real
**5** (six files carry the line, `tl-classic/.../execSetResultsJS.inc.tpl` does not exist);
the audit join moved below the tcversion proof; the ownership walk now asserts the first
node is `node_type_id = 3`; the status class is validated and `removeClass()` no longer
strips the badge box's own classes. Suite `Issue #1807` in
  `tmp/TLU_Test_Cases.md` - 34/34 PASS.

### KEY BUGFIX — #1685: `reqTreeReorder.html` — drag & drop did not mark the form dirty (Refs #1681)

- [KEY BUGFIX] - #1685: `reqTreeReorder.html` — drag-and-drop reordering did not show the "Unsaved changes" chip (asymmetry with button path); fixed by calling `markDirty()` after render in the drop handler. Verified: chip appears on drop, clears on Apply/Discard, Apply persists order. No API change.

### KEY BUGFIX — #1688: `reqTreeReorder.html` — the toolbar stayed live on the 403/404 error page (Refs #1681)

- [KEY BUGFIX] - #1688: `reqTreeReorder.html` — on the 403/404 error page the toolbar stayed fully live: **Apply order answered "Nothing to apply" on a page the user may not even see**. Root cause was a *missing transition*, not a wrong condition: `idleUI()` owns the enabled/disabled state of every toolbar control but was only ever called from the **success** handler of `action=init`, while all six error entry points (403 / 404 / 405 / any other status / no project in context / bad payload) end in `showState()`, which merely hid the three cards. The toolbar sits outside the cards `showState()` hides, so on a dead page the six controls stayed enabled over an empty client-side model. The toolbar fix itself was already on the default branch as `571760ea4` (Refs #1681): a page-level `DEAD` flag deliberately kept **outside** `GRANT` so a 403 can never be mistaken for "read-only"; `showState()` sets `DEAD` **and calls `idleUI()`**; `hideState()` clears it; `idleUI()` folds `DEAD` into its condition and suppresses both the read-only banner ("you may view but not modify" is the wrong message on a page you cannot see at all) and the drag-and-drop hint; the `#specSel` read-only re-enable is gated on `!DEAD`; and `applyOrder()` / `doMove()` / `discard()` bail out early so the dead state is enforced in the handlers and not only visually. Re-measuring it surfaced a **residual hole in the same class**, now also fixed here: `DEAD` was never folded into the *row* controls, so on a success -> dead transition (`init` succeeded once, a later `load()` failed) `nudge()` still reordered the list and lit the "Unsaved changes" chip, `.pickbtn` still armed a Move that could never be submitted, and the drop handler still accepted the gesture — the rows survived the transition and `#ordCard` merely hid them. `applyRowState()` now carries a `DEAD` term for `.rm`, `[data-mv]` and `.pickbtn` and clears `draggable`/the grip icon, `showState()` ends with `applyRowState()`, and `nudge()`, the `.pickbtn` handler and the `drop` handler all bail out on `DEAD`. Suites `Issue #1688` and `Issue #1688 (addendum)` in `tmp/TLU_Test_Cases.md` - 21/21 PASS (403 / 404 / `MISSING_TPROJECT` / 405 measured, plus a forced success -> dead transition and an explicit no-regression pass on the success path); `events` clean. No API change.

### KEY BUGFIX — #1690: `reqTreeReorder.html` — "Back to specification management" pointed at the WRONG test project (Refs #1681)

- [KEY BUGFIX] - #1690: `reqTreeReorder.html` — the **Back to specification management** link sent the user to an unrelated test project: `#1686` removed `href="#"` by baking a real target into the **static** markup, and the id it used — `tproject_id=13` — was the fixture project the screen was developed on. `reqTreeReorder.html` is not PHP and is served to every project, so the literal id was correct for exactly one of them (measured: header *Project 60*, link `?tproject_id=13`, landing on `"Project 13 - Requirement Specification Management"`). The corrective commit `1ba5015e7` (Refs #1681) is correct — markup fallback is the id-less, in-app `/gui/templates/requirements/reqSpecMgmt.html` and `buildBackLink()` rewrites it **synchronously at DOM-ready, before `TLi18n.load()` and before the `init` AJAX call**, so no 403/404 and no slow API can degrade it — and this run re-measured it on the success, 404 and missing-context paths (all correct) and closed the issue with that evidence. Verifying it exposed a **residual hole in the same control**, now fixed here (commit `d66f0feb7`): the id-less fallback is "valid" but not *usable* on the one state it exists for (`load()`'s `MISSING_TPROJECT`): `reqSpecMgmt.html` reads the project from the URL only (`:495`) and has **no project selector**, so it renders `Test Project: -` with every dropdown empty — the escape hatch of a dead end was a second dead end. `buildBackLink()` now resolves the project as URL param (unchanged, always wins, so the verified paths are bit-identical) -> **same-origin** `document.referrer`'s `tproject_id` -> the id-less link. The origin check is conservative and unit-measured in-page: `https://evil.example.com/x?tproject_id=99` and `http://127.0.0.1:8082/...?tproject_id=60` both yield `0`, so the link still can never point at a foreign project; `?tproject_id=abc` (`TPROJECT_ID === NaN`) correctly falls through to the referrer instead of poisoning the href. Purely client-side and still synchronous — no new i18n key, no API change, no schema change. Suite `Issue #1690` in `tmp/TLU_Test_Cases.md` (9 cases, 9/9 PASS, incl. a live reorder that still persists `nodes_hierarchy.node_order`) + fixture `tmp/fixtures_1690.sql` (two test projects 13/60, since the defect is invisible on the hardcoded one); details in `docs/Bugfix-Issue-1690-reqtreereorder-Back-Link-Wrong-Project.md`.

### KEY BUGFIX — #1821: `cfieldsTprojectAssign.html` — "Check / uncheck all" was completely dead (Refs #1816)

- [KEY BUGFIX] - #1821: `cfieldsTprojectAssign.html` — the **Check / uncheck all** master checkbox in both table headers was dead: no row ticked, no `(n)` counter, Assign/Unassign stayed disabled, one console `TypeError` per click. `toggleAll()` called `invalidateSearch()` **ahead of** the loop that does the ticking, so the exception aborted the function; the method was removed in DataTables 1.11 and the screen loads 1.13.7 from CDN, so it exists on no receiver and the statement was **deleted** rather than relocated (commits `0df8f57f7` + `d9969d57a`). Suite `Issue #1821` (TC-1821-01..12, 12/12 PASS) + fixture `tmp/fixtures_1821.php`; details in `docs/Bugfix-Issue-1821-cfieldsTprojectAssign-CheckUncheckAll.md`. No API change, no i18n change.

### KEY BUGFIX — #1829: `reqTreeReorder.html` — the edge-row Up/Down tooltips leaked the raw i18n KEY instead of a string (Refs #1824)

- [KEY BUGFIX] - #1829: `reqTreeReorder.html` — the disabled **Up** / **To top** (first row) and **Down** / **To bottom** (last row) buttons showed the raw key `reqtr.alreadyFirst` / `reqtr.alreadyLast` as their `title` attribute, in every locale. Root cause: `TLi18n.t()` returns the KEY itself for a bundle entry that does not exist (`gui/templates/i18n/i18n.js:175`, `var str = _strings[key] || key;`), so the `t(key) || 'Already the first requirement'` fallback could never fire — the key is truthy, so it won and the English literal was unreachable dead code. Fix: the two keys now exist in all 10 locale bundles (`reqtr.alreadyFirst` / `reqtr.alreadyLast`, translated per locale) and `btn()` resolves them through the new `edgeTitle()` helper, which routes the lookup through the module's own `TLi18n.has()` existence check, guards against a present-but-blank value, and omits the attribute instead of leaking a key; the unreachable literals were deleted (hardcoded strings were a rule-3 violation). Measured: `title` = "Already the first/last requirement" (en), "Deja este prima/ultima cerință" (ro), "Bereits die erste/letzte Anforderung" (de); non-edge rows stay `title=null`; with `has()` forced false or `t()` forced to echo the key the tooltip is omitted and no `reqtr.*` string reaches the DOM. Known limitation: `.rm.dis, .rm:disabled { pointer-events: none }` (reqTreeReorder.html:54, from `4f074a2b5`/#1804) keeps the disabled edge buttons out of the hover chain, so the tooltip is reachable through the accessibility tree / DOM `title` but not by mouse hover. No API change.
